/* The original look shared by every document: the original's text, its menu cursor and its button prompts.

   Scale: one pixel of the original is 3dp (its 240 lines over the UI's 720 dp), so every size below is a whole
   number of original pixels times 3. The text is the original-look family (the bitmap font or the modern one, as the
   player's text_font setting picks), whose 11-pixel cells keep their shape at 33dp, three times.

   Sources: the colours of the text are the font's CLUT and TEXT_COLORS (font.c: drawString): #d0d0d0 the ink,
   #fff371 the yellow of headings, #288ad0 blue, #b2b2b2 the grey of descriptions. */

body {
	font-family: "Pixel Body";
	font-weight: normal;
	font-size: 30dp;
	color: #d8f1ff;
}

/* Text that the original-look sheets style: the pixel skins draw it with the pixel fonts, and so
   does this one — accented letters and ñ read far better here than in the bitmap font (its cells
   clip the accents) and the type stays one system: Pixel Body for everything the page writes.
   The size is 30dp on purpose: Pixel Body's cell is 15 pixels, so it rasterizes without
   horizontal bands only at whole multiples of it (30dp = two dp per pixel). The sheets still ask
   for the bitmap font's 33dp and 22dp here and there, and at those sizes the pixel font's rows
   land between the screen's pixels — the letters come out striped. 30dp everywhere fixes the
   sampling without touching the boxes, which were sized for the taller 33. No !important: the style
   parser rejects it and drops the whole declaration. */
.original-look,
.original-look.original-dialogue {
	font-family: "Pixel Body";
	font-weight: normal;
	font-size: 30dp;
	line-height: 33dp;
	color: #d8f1ff;
}

/* An entry of a menu: plain text with the original's selector, a 1-pixel outline round the focused row
   (main_menu.c: renderInitialMenu, #A0A000). No hover fill and no animation: the original has neither. The rows are
   the original's 12 pixels apart (36dp); its selector is 14 pixels tall and overlaps the rows next to it, which
   would let a focused row overlap its neighbours and stop the keyboard's up and down from reaching them, so here the
   outline is the row's own 12 pixels: a documented delta of 2 pixels. */
button {
	padding: 0 9dp;
	margin: 0;
	border-radius: 0;
	border: 3dp transparent;
	color: #d8f1ff;
	background-color: transparent;
	line-height: 34dp;
	transition: none;
}

button:focus {
	border-color: #ffd51e;
	background-color: #153653;
	color: #ffe64a;
}

button:active {
	background-color: #244764;
}

/* A button prompt: the pixel skin's key cap — the name in Pixel Small, 2dp per pixel of the
   font, the cap 34dp tall with room around the word so no letter touches its edges. */
.key {
	font-family: "Pixel Small";
	min-width: 26dp;
	height: 34dp;
	padding: 0 9dp;
	margin-right: 9dp;
	border-radius: 0;
	border: 2dp #90d9fa;
	font-weight: normal;
	font-size: 26dp;
	line-height: 30dp;
	color: #d8f1ff;
	background-color: #102a40cc;
}

/* Button glyphs (../../common/base.rcss): the engine's own shape art inside the cap, like the
   pixel skin. The bitmap font's characters and the animated frames are left out — over the
   pixel caps they drew a second glyph behind the word's letters. */
.glyph-char,
.glyph-frame {
	display: none;
}

.family-shapes .glyph-art,
.family-positions .glyph-art {
	display: inline-block;
	width: 20dp;
	height: 20dp;
}

.key.family-shapes,
.key.family-positions {
	min-width: 34dp;
	padding: 0 6dp;
	margin-right: 9dp;
	border-width: 2dp;
	font-size: 26dp;
}

.key.glyph-face {
	min-width: 34dp;
	padding: 0 6dp;
	border-radius: 0;
}

/* The original's window (ui.c: renderUIBoxStatic, renderUIBoxBorder), for any document's box: the fill #2D3840 at
   half opacity (the semi-transparent mode), the three- and four-line border the original-box decorator draws (black,
   light cyan #90D9FA and dark cyan #4A9FC5 lines, each one original pixel, 3dp) and the rounded corner sprites of
   SYSTEM_W. The border's 3dp keep the fill off the outer black line. A box with an opaque fill sets its own
   background-color; one that adds a decorator repeats this list with its own last (RmlUi paints the first decorator on
   top, as CSS does with backgrounds, so a fill listed first hides the border). Opening and closing animate with
   ui/common/original_box.lua. */
.original-box {
	border: 3dp transparent;
	border-radius: 0;
	background-color: #0b1c2de8;
	decorator: original-box(3dp) border-box, image(box-tl scale-none left top) border-box, image(box-tr scale-none right top) border-box,
		image(box-bl scale-none left bottom) border-box, image(box-br scale-none right bottom) border-box;
}
